      ":question/id": "4211b952-74e4-50f5-a084-41dbfc1c4910",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y^{2}y' - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot y^{5} = 3 \\cdot y^{4}$\n- **II.** $yy' - 3 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sqrt{y} = 4 \\cdot x^{2}$\n- **III.** $y' + 4 \\cdot y \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = 5 \\cdot x \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\ny' - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot y^{3} = 3 \\cdot y^{2}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny' - \\frac{3 \\cdot x}{\\sqrt{y}} = \\frac{4 \\cdot x^{2}}{y}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\nTherefore, select none of the given equations.",
